course: dictionary
lesson: 14308
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word nuts can mean food, a small metal piece, something crazy, or liking something very much. Context tells you which meaning is right. Read the words around it and ask: which meaning fits here?
explain: «The squirrel buried nuts under the oak tree to eat in winter.» Here nuts are food, the hard-shelled fruits or seeds of some trees. The squirrel and the word buried are your clues.
explain: «Dad tightened the nuts on the bike wheel with a spanner.» Here nuts are small metal pieces with a hole in the middle, which screw onto bolts. The spanner and the word tightened are your clues.
explain: «That idea is nuts!» and «Sam is nuts about football» are informal. Nuts means crazy or silly in the first, and liking something very much in the second. To check, swap in another word such as "crazy" or "very keen". If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Nuts is the plural of nut.
fact: A nut can be a hard-shelled fruit or seed, such as a walnut, hazelnut or almond, and many can be eaten.
fact: A nut can also be a small metal piece with a hole in the middle that screws onto a bolt.
fact: Nuts (informal) means crazy or silly, and nuts about something means liking it very much.
fact: Some people have a serious nut allergy, so many schools are nut-free, and you should never share food with someone who has an allergy.
exercise: «The squirrel buried nuts under the oak tree to eat in winter.» What are the nuts in this sentence? | Pieces of a football | *Hard-shelled fruits or seeds that can be eaten | Small metal pieces for a bike | Crazy ideas
exercise: «Dad tightened the nuts on the bolts with a spanner.» What are the nuts here? | Parts of a sandwich | Silly people | Hard-shelled fruits that can be eaten | *Small metal pieces with a hole in the middle
exercise: Ella says, «Our school is nut-free because some children have a serious allergy.» What does nut-free mean? | Everyone must eat nuts every day | The school has lots of squirrels | *No foods with nuts in them are allowed | The food is all made of metal
test: «That plan is nuts!» What does nuts mean here? | Made of metal | *Crazy or silly | Very tasty | Full of nut shells
test: «Priya is nuts about swimming.» What does nuts about mean? | Hates the pool | *Likes it very much | Is tired of it | Is afraid of water
exam: Mia says, «Let's go over the nuts and bolts of the plan before we start.» What does nuts and bolts mean here? | Crazy ideas | Hard-shelled seeds | Small metal pieces for a bike | *The basic details
